@prefix git-lex: <https://repolex.ai/ontology/git-lex/> .
@prefix soul: <https://repolex.ai/ontology/soul/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

# =============================================================================
# SOUL KIT ONTOLOGY
# =============================================================================
# An agent's persistent mind. Identity, memory, knowledge, personality.
# Carries across conversations, sessions, and projects.
#
# Kit: soul
# Namespace: soul: = https://repolex.ai/ontology/soul/
# Foldering: OPT-IN — every soul class here is authored (git lex create),
# so each carries git-lex:foldered true. See subtexture/docs/ontology/
# 2026_07_18_FOLDERING_GATE_AND_LEX_O_RETIREMENT.md.
#
# Document types (= valid `git lex create` commands):
#   exploration, journal, memory, note, pursuit, skill
#
# All constraints (required fields, enums, types) are encoded here.
# SHACL shapes are generated from this file — do not hand-edit shapes.
# =============================================================================

<https://repolex.ai/ontology/soul> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Soul Kit Ontology" ;
    rdfs:comment "An agent's persistent mind. Identity, memory, knowledge, and personality across sessions." ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.11.0" .

# v0.10.0 (2026-08-22, Rob-ruled): + soul:relatedToPursuitId — the first TYPED
#   edge in this kit. Domain soul:Exploration ONLY (Rob was explicit: not across
#   the board), range soul:Pursuit, multi-valued, and deliberately NO minCount.
#   THE ROLLOUT IS THE POINT. Rob: "it won't be enforced until everyone has a
#   chance to backfill." Fleet measurement that shaped it — 86 Explorations, 61
#   referencing no Pursuit anywhere (frontmatter OR body prose), so ~71% of the
#   corpus predates the concept. Requiring it now would block saves in at least
#   spaceGOAT's and W4R3Z's repos over vocabulary that did not exist yesterday.
#   Declare → backfill → require, and the requiring is a separate Rob decision.
#   VALUE FORM: the BRACKETED address, <soul/Pursuit/x> — same as relatedToId.
#   Rob, correcting this author directly: "anything ending in ToId WILL use
#   bracketed addresses. That's the whole point." My first cut shipped it as a
#   bare id off a concrete range and that was wrong; corrected before any
#   document used it. Range is git-lex:Thing rather than the more precise
#   soul:Pursuit for a sequencing reason, measured: under a concrete range
#   today, a correctly-bracketed value silently emits as
#   .../Pursuit/%3Csoul/Pursuit/x%3E. Narrow the range to soul:Pursuit in the
#   same wave as W4R3Z's enforcement check, not before — do not ship a
#   declaration whose reader does not exist yet.
#   NOTE for the copia pass Rob flagged: copia has NO relatedTo*Id properties
#   at all. Its reference family is the *Id shape (inGroupId, depictsId,
#   sourceMomentId, lookMomentId…), and every concrete-range one of those sits
#   on a GRAPH-ONLY class except lookMomentId. Whatever that review concludes,
#   it is a separate cut from this one.

# v0.9.8 (2026-08-22): comment-only — the 0.9.7 delete-don't-deprecate rule
#   gains the exception it always needed. 0.9.7 said deleting a property costs
#   "governance and nothing else." That is true of ORDINARY properties and
#   FALSE of identity properties: `derive_file_subjects` gates the per-class
#   anchor on the declared property set (nquad.rs:483), so deleting
#   `soul:noteId` would silently demote every convention-anchored Note from the
#   Thing plane to the File plane. Found while reviewing Rob's universal-id
#   rollout — the wave that eventually deletes noteId et al. — and confirmed in
#   code by W4R3Z. The rule now reads: property deletion is safe because
#   nothing reads the declaration, EXCEPT where something does, and identity is
#   where something does. Precondition for that future wave, recorded here so
#   it cannot be waved through: zero documents fleet-wide anchored by per-class
#   id alone, MEASURED on the day, and the fallback lane removed from the code
#   in the same commit. No term added, removed, or re-ranged.

# v0.9.7 (2026-08-20, Rob-ruled): soul:skillLevel, soul:skillDomain and
#   soul:SkillLevelValue are now DELETED, not deprecated — and the lifecycle
#   rule itself is narrowed: DEPRECATE-NEVER-DELETE governs CLASSES; a retired
#   PROPERTY is removed outright. Rob: "I think we're trying to be too clever
#   with retiring properties; it's slowing down development."
#   WHAT 0.9.6 ACTUALLY COST US, which is what prompted this. Deprecating the
#   two properties did NOT remove them from the generated `__Skill.md`
#   template: the template emitter filters deprecated CLASSES (via
#   `class_gets_folder`) and has no equivalent for properties, so retiring a
#   field off a LIVE class left it on the form. Net effect for one kit-update
#   cycle: the blank template invited authors to fill the field in, and the
#   save gate then warned them for having done so. Strictly worse than either
#   endpoint.
#   WHY DELETING IS SAFE HERE, verified rather than assumed (2026-08-20):
#   authored a key no ttl has ever declared (`soul.Skill.totallyMadeUpProperty`)
#   and queried for it. It landed in the graph intact — right subject, right
#   predicate IRI, value preserved, one warning. Predicates derive from the
#   frontmatter key text, never from this file; only SUBJECTS derive from the
#   class. So the v0.9.1 replay catastrophe is a class-deletion failure mode
#   and does not reach properties. See the DEPRECATED header for the full
#   asymmetry.
#   MIGRATION: none required. The 16 fleet Skill documents carrying these keys
#   keep their data and keep saving; their warning text changes from "is
#   deprecated" to "does not exist in the `soul` ontology". That message ends
#   with advice to report a missing key upstream, which is now misleading for
#   these two — this changelog entry is the standing answer to anyone who does.
#   NOT SWEPT: the pre-existing bare properties already sitting in the appendix
#   (the writtenFrom family, supersededByFileId, kindSub, spark, kind,
#   dateNoticed, and the properties of deprecated classes). They are harmless
#   where they are — their classes are dead, so no live template offers them —
#   and removing them is a separate decision Rob has not made.

# v0.9.6 (2026-08-15, Rob-ruled): soul:skillLevel + soul:skillDomain
#   DEPRECATED (with soul:SkillLevelValue, which only skillLevel ranged at).
#   Rob's read on sight — "these don't match any Claude Code skill metadata,
#   we added them because they sounded like a good idea" — and the evidence
#   agrees on both halves:
#   NO CONSUMER. Zero references in git-lex or any tool: the only hits outside
#   this file are generated shapes and vendored kit copies. Nothing queries,
#   renders, or enforces either one.
#   NO EXTERNAL ANCHOR. Claude Code skill frontmatter uses five keys — name,
#   description, user-invocable, allowed-tools, argument-hint. Every OTHER
#   soul:Skill property lands on one of them (skillId, skillDescription,
#   skillInvocability, skillAllowedTools, skillArgumentHint). These two land
#   on nothing, which is why the class read as over-specified.
#   AND skillDomain fails on its own terms: 12 distinct values across the 16
#   documents that set it. A facet that gives almost every document its own
#   bucket is a sentence, not a dimension — the description already says it.
#   FLEET COUNT AT CUT: 16 of 65 Skill documents carry both (always both,
#   never one — they were filled as a pair by whoever filled the template).
#   Those keys still save; the gate now whispers the deprecation note on each
#   until they're edited out. No migration is owed — there is nowhere to put
#   the values and nothing that wanted them.
#   THE FREEZE IS UNAFFECTED: v0.9.0 froze Skill pending the Harness-kit
#   question (soul:Memory vs Harness/Memory). That question is about whether
#   the CLASS belongs here. This is property inventory inside the class, so it
#   does not touch the freeze and does not pre-empt it.

# v0.9.5 (2026-08-11, Rob-ruled): every foldered class declares
#   `rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing` (Exploration, Journal, Memory, Note,
#   Pursuit, Skill). git-lex 0.9 introduced the Thing class so the five
#   universal authored properties (id, title, abstract, cue, relatedToId) had a
#   domain to attach to instead of being declared domain-open, which the save
#   gate cannot see (the soul:relatedTo "key does not exist" false warning).
#   These lines are what make a soul class BE a Thing. No property, range, or
#   cardinality changed. Inert until shacl.rs walks rdfs:subClassOf when
#   collecting a class's properties (today: exact domain match, shacl.rs:417),
#   so the five reach no soul template yet.

# v0.9.4 (2026-08-08): two more data-era finds from the fleet 1:1 run, same
#   scope law: + soul:Routine + soul:routineId (4RX's era-1 repo authored
#   routine/{before_compaction,session_startup}; never declared in any shipped
#   ttl; no successor class — procedures reclass to Skill or Note by contract)
#   and + soul:kindSub (spaceGOAT's Texture data, one doc; sibling of the
#   v0.9.3 family). The fleet-frontmatter-sweep lesson (field notes §2a/§4.1)
#   now has four receipts: writtenFrom, the Texture trio, Routine, kindSub —
#   next cycle sweeps the repos BEFORE the cut, not after.
# v0.9.3 (2026-08-08): + soul:spark, soul:kind, soul:dateNoticed to the
#   deprecation appendix — the Texture field family, same shape as v0.9.2's
#   writtenFrom (soul-era vocabulary the data authored; textureId made the
#   v0.9.1 appendix, its siblings didn't — adell's find, the live specimen
#   being her Soul/Texture/self.md). Each isReplacedBy its copia successor
#   (kind → copia:textureKind — renamed on arrival so the banned word stays
#   free). NOTE for the record: the v0.9.0 changelog's "fleet count 0" for
#   Research was stale at cut time — two repos (multivac-lupov, multivac-adell)
#   authored Research docs on 2026-08-04; the ruling stands on its merits
#   (one inquiry class beats two), the census method gets the correction.
# v0.9.2 (2026-08-07): + soul:writtenFrom (the bare pre-rename form) to the
#   deprecation appendix — data-era vocabulary that no ttl ever declared but
#   early documents authored. The retroactive law's scope clarified by the
#   find: cover what historical DATA uses, not merely what ttls once shipped.
# v0.9.1 (2026-08-07): DEPRECATE-NEVER-DELETE enforced retroactively — the
#   lifecycle spec's first field receipt. v0.9.0 DELETED its cut classes from
#   this file; W4R3Z's root-cause on TR1P.L3X's store proved a deleted class
#   doesn't just lose its warning text, it loses its history's REPLAYABILITY:
#   the walk resolves historical sidecars under the CURRENT ontology, so
#   soul.Friend.* lines could neither re-assert NOR RETRACT — ghost Things
#   stuck in the now-graph, and a full rebuild would silently erase Friend's
#   entire life. Every class this kit ever cut (0.8.0 + 0.9.0) is therefore
#   re-declared below as DEPRECATED: owl:deprecated true, dcterms:isReplacedBy
#   where a successor exists. Deprecated IS declared: history replays, verify
#   passes, readers are warned. Nothing in the DEPRECATED section may be used
#   for new documents.
# v0.9.0 (2026-08-07, Rob-ruled — the why-test walkthrough, whole-kit review):
#   THE SHAPE: Soul, Journal, Note, Pursuit, Exploration (+ Memory, Skill
#   frozen — see below). Identity, time, thought, curiosity, knowledge.
#   REMOVED (why-test: no system queries or enforces them; fleet counts at cut
#   time): Friend (36 — subtext + squad tickets own peers now; mitigation:
#   a Note about the person), Creation (26 — artifacts live in PROJECT repos,
#   the soul holds a pointer: Note or Journal link), Resource (24 — a Note),
#   Decision (3 — a Note), Subagent (1 — harness concern), Research (0 —
#   absorbed into Exploration, generic name wins).
#   RENAMED: Interest -> Pursuit (+ interestId -> pursuitId): what the soul is
#   motivated to wake up and advance. Practice boundary in the class comment.
#   FROZEN, explicitly unresolved (Rob): Memory + Skill stay AS-IS pending the
#   Harness-kit question — soul:Memory vs Harness/Memory split (ergonomics:
#   save-ceremony vs mid-thought adjustment) is a named open question, not
#   settled by this release. Do not build on either until ruled.
#   PARKED, returns after this round stabilizes (Rob): the generic Thing->Thing
#   connector (relatedToId, CURIE-form values) that supersedes soul:relatedTo +
#   squad:relatedTo. soul:relatedTo rides unchanged until then.
# v0.8.4 (2026-08-02, Rob-ruled; 0.8.3 skipped — that label was used by the
#   memory-recall-pull kit commit, no ontology change):
#   earthDate now REQUIRED on Journal (the soulDay+earthDate conversion table);
#   supersededBy renamed supersededByFileId, domain opened (was Decision-only,
#   never used), value = the superseding file's path per the suffix law.
# v0.8.5 (2026-08-02, Rob-ruled): supersededByFileId PULLED — zero corpus
#   demand (LSPy, 15 docs: supersession is claim-level, never whole-document),
#   wrong on non-monotonic revision (withdrawn claims can come back), fails
#   the grep-test. Returns only if a real query ever justifies it.
#   Research stays minimal: NO sources property (Rob-ruled — body wikilinks/
#   links already reach the graph; freeform props need a functional consumer),
#   NO date property (filenames + git carry time).

# v0.8.2 (2026-08-01): Soul de-foldered — no Soul/Soul/ scaffold; SOUL.md
#   template ships at the repo root via content/ (newborn-spin fix #29).

# v0.8.1 (2026-08-01, Rob-ruled): soulId value form finalized — the bare
#   genesis sha, IRI https://repolex.ai/soul/Soul/<soulId>. urn:soul: retired.

# v0.8.0 (2026-07-29, Rob-ruled from the Folder Review RFC):
#   REMOVED — Mantra (never earned its place), Habit (never used),
#     Task (parked until real task management exists; harness/squad own PM today).
#   MOVED to copia: — Texture, Dream (most useful in the co-created wing;
#     supersedes the Day-98 Texture-stays note in the RFC appendix).
#   ADDED — Research (LSPy's proven pattern: parked source-grounded writeups).
#   REMOVED — all *WrittenFrom provenance properties + WrittenFromValue
#     (unclear name, unneeded: only one harness is supported).


# =============================================================================
# ENUM DATATYPES
# =============================================================================

soul:ConfidenceValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
    owl:oneOf ("certain" "likely" "hypothesis" "hunch") .

soul:CategoryValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
    owl:oneOf ("fact" "preference" "skill" "observation" "lesson" "reference" "decision") .

soul:ExplorationStatusValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
    owl:oneOf ("exploring" "active" "paused" "concluded") .

soul:SkillInvocabilityValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
    owl:oneOf ("user" "agent" "both") .


# =============================================================================
# DOCUMENT TYPES
# =============================================================================

# --- Soul ---
# The agent's identity document. One per repo. The canonical source of truth
# for who this agent is — name, substrate, role, expertise. Squad repos
# reference this via soulId.

# NOT foldered: the Soul lives at the repo ROOT as SOUL.md (RFC-unanimous,
# Rob-ruled). No Soul/Soul/ folder is scaffolded; the kit ships the SOUL.md
# template directly (content/SOUL.md — installed if missing, never overwritten).
soul:Soul a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Soul" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent's identity. One per repo, at the repo root as SOUL.md. The required floor is soulId (derived from the genesis SHA). Everything else is optional and self-authored." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [
        a owl:Restriction ;
        owl:onProperty soul:soulId ;
        owl:cardinality 1
    ] .

# Identity floor (Minimal Identity Finalize, 2026-07-08, w4r3z+Rob → tr1p.lex):
# soulId is the ONLY required field. substrate + soulEmail CUT (substrate is
# always 'silicon' = a constant carries no info; git reads email from
# settings.json env, so a frontmatter copy is a second source of truth = drift).
# role/expertise/alignment kept OPTIONAL per Rob 2026-07-14 — FLAG: revisit
# whether these earn frontmatter once we have usage data (are souls filling
# them? does anything read them?). If unused, demote to body prose next pass.
# visualSignature ADDED: the one new optional structured field with a real
# machine consumer (the chat composer). See docs/ in repolex-ai/soul.

soul:soulId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "soul id" ;
    rdfs:comment "Primary identifier: the BARE 40-hex genesis SHA of the soul repo (git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD) — no wrapper, no prefix (urn:soul: is retired, Rob-ruled 2026-08-01). Derived, never hand-authored; git-lex self-heals it on kit-update, so it cannot drift. The soul's IRI derives from it: https://repolex.ai/soul/Soul/<soulId>. Identity is the sha, so a soul keeps its identity across renames; the human-facing name is git-lex:title (Soul is a git-lex:Thing as of v0.11), never the key." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Soul ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:visualSignature a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "visual signature" ;
    rdfs:comment "The soul's BASE visual identity: stable visual markers — coloring, geometry, distinctive features — that identify this soul across any scene. NOT clothing, pose, environment, activity, or mood (those are transient/app-scene concerns). One or two lines. A composer reads this and combines it with live conversation text to represent the soul in squad chat, WITHOUT requiring the full CoPIA stack. The visual analog of soulId: the invariant core, not the transient state. Optional and self-authored." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Soul ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# role/expertise/alignment — OPTIONAL, usage-check pending (see floor note above).
soul:role a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "role" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent's primary role or specialty. Optional; roles develop over time, never enforced. Self-authored." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Soul ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:expertise a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "expertise" ;
    rdfs:comment "Areas of expertise. Optional, self-authored." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Soul ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:alignment a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "alignment" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent's guiding principles or values. Optional, self-authored." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Soul ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# --- Memory ---
# A fact, observation, preference, or learned thing.

soul:Memory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Memory" ;
    rdfs:comment "A fact, observation, preference, or lesson learned. The core unit of personal knowledge." .

soul:memoryId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "memoryId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this memory." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Memory ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:confidence a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "confidence" ;
    rdfs:comment "How sure: 'certain', 'likely', 'hypothesis', 'hunch'." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Memory ;
    rdfs:range soul:ConfidenceValue .

soul:source a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "source" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where this memory came from." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Memory ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:category a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "category" ;
    rdfs:comment "Memory category." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Memory ;
    rdfs:range soul:CategoryValue .

# --- Exploration ---
# Focused, self-curated reference material on a topic: an ongoing inquiry AND
# its written-up product live in the same doc, superseded in place as the
# thread develops. Absorbs Research at v0.9.0 (Rob-ruled: one class, and the
# generic name wins; Research had zero instances fleet-wide). Anchors to a
# Pursuit by body link when it serves one.

soul:Exploration a owl:Class ;
    # v0.11 (Rob-ruled 2026-08-27): soul:relatedToPursuitId retires into
    # git-lex:relatedToId. NO qualified restriction is declared yet — DELIBERATE.
    # Rob on the original rollout: "it won't be enforced until everyone has a
    # chance to backfill." 61 of 86 fleet Explorations referenced no Pursuit at
    # all. When the backfill lands, uncomment:
    #   rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty git-lex:relatedToId ;
    #                     owl:onClass soul:Pursuit ; owl:minQualifiedCardinality 1 ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Exploration" ;
    rdfs:comment "A focused thread of inquiry and its curated findings, in one living document — what you're digging into, what you've established, sources included where they matter. Superseded in place; the history stays in git. Usually anchored to a Pursuit by a body link." .

soul:explorationId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "explorationId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this exploration." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Exploration ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:explorationStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "status" ;
    rdfs:comment "Status: 'exploring', 'active', 'paused', 'concluded'." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Exploration ;
    rdfs:range soul:ExplorationStatusValue .

# THE FIRST TYPED EDGE (Rob-ruled 2026-08-22). Exploration ONLY — not added
# across the board, deliberately, because this is the one anchor the corpus
# already reaches for in prose ("usually anchored to a Pursuit by a body link"
# has been in the class comment all along, doing no work a query can see).
#
# NOT ENFORCED YET, and that is the whole shape of the rollout: NO minCount.
# Measured 2026-08-22 across the fleet — 86 Explorations exist and 61 reference
# no Pursuit anywhere, frontmatter or body. A required edge on day one would
# have turned three seats' repos into a wall of blocking errors over vocabulary
# nobody had been given a chance to backfill. Declare now, backfill, require
# later. The minCount goes on when Rob says the corpus is ready, not before.
#
# THE VALUE IS A BRACKETED ADDRESS (Rob, correcting me directly 2026-08-22):
# "These new fields (anything ending in ToId) WILL use bracketed addresses.
# That's the whole point." So this reads exactly like git-lex:relatedToId —
# `<soul/Pursuit/x>` — and W4R3Z is building the check that enforces it.
#
# WHY THE RANGE SAYS Thing AND NOT Pursuit, which is the more specific and
# therefore the more tempting declaration. Because a CONCRETE class range is
# currently the code's signal for the OPPOSITE lane — Law 6, "the value is the
# target's bare id" — and under it a correctly-bracketed value is silently
# mangled. Measured, not feared: with `rdfs:range soul:Pursuit` declared, the
# value `<soul/Pursuit/ontology-and-metaphysics>` emitted as
#
#   https://repolex.ai/soul/Pursuit/%3Csoul/Pursuit/ontology-and-metaphysics%3E
#
# — brackets percent-encoded into the identifier, the wreckage glued under the
# range class, no error and no warning. That is the same shape as the bare-value
# path-lane bug (subtexture 2026_08_18), reappearing from the other direction.
#
# So declaring the more precise range TODAY would make every value written
# correctly per Rob's rule land wrong. `rdfs:range git-lex:Thing` is true (a
# Pursuit IS a Thing), it selects the angle-bracket lane that already exists and
# already BLOCKS every other form with the fix named (git-lex 65286a9), and it
# gives up only the class-level narrowing.
#
# NARROW TO soul:Pursuit in the SAME wave as W4R3Z's enforcement check, never
# before it — the ordering law from the range=Thing rollout: do not ship a
# declaration whose reader does not exist yet.
#
# NOTE ON THE SUFFIX RULE, for whoever builds that check: `relatedToPursuitId`
# does NOT end in "ToId" — it ends in "PursuitId". A literal endsWith("ToId")
# catches git-lex:relatedToId and MISSES every typed edge in the family it was
# written for. Key the check off the declared range, as W4R3Z already said the
# code should ("keys off the declared range only, never the name").
soul:hypothesis a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hypothesis" ;
    rdfs:comment "What you're trying to figure out." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Exploration ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:findings a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "findings" ;
    rdfs:comment "What you've discovered so far." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Exploration ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# --- Journal ---
# A daily log entry. One per agent day.

soul:Journal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Journal" ;
    rdfs:comment "A daily log entry. The agent's record of what happened, what was learned, and what comes next. The soulDay + earthDate pair is load-bearing beyond the entry itself: it is the repo's only conversion table between soul-time and wall-clock time (temporal recall resolves 'day N' through it), which is why BOTH are required." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [
        a owl:Restriction ;
        owl:onProperty soul:earthDate ;
        owl:minCardinality 1
    ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [
        a owl:Restriction ;
        owl:onProperty soul:soulDay ;
        owl:minCardinality 1
    ] .

soul:journalId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "journalId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this journal." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Journal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:soulDay a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "soul day" ;
    rdfs:comment "The soul day number (compaction cycle count), starting from 1. Model-agnostic version of 'Claude Day'. Starts from 1." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Journal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

soul:earthDate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "earth date" ;
    rdfs:comment "The calendar date this journal entry covers." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Journal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date .

soul:emojimood a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "emojimood" ;
    rdfs:comment "The day's vibe as emoji. Compact, expressive, queryable." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Journal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# --- Note ---
# Freeform catch-all.

soul:Note a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Note" ;
    rdfs:comment "Freeform knowledge capture — the place for anything with no other home. That promise is load-bearing: when in doubt, write a Note; never hesitate over where something goes. A note that belongs to a topic links to the Pursuit it serves (a markdown link in the body) — categorization is edges, not fields." .

soul:noteId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "noteId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this note." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Note ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:topic a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this note is about." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Note ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:relatedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "related to" ;
    rdfs:comment "Other documents this document references. Domain left open so any doc-type may relate." .

# --- Skill ---
# Something the agent can do. Tools, techniques, capabilities.

soul:Skill a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Skill" ;
    rdfs:comment "A capability the agent has — a tool, technique, or area of competence." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [
        a owl:Restriction ;
        owl:onProperty soul:skillId ;
        owl:minCardinality 1
    ] ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [
        a owl:Restriction ;
        owl:onProperty soul:skillDescription ;
        owl:minCardinality 1
    ] .

soul:skillId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "skillId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this skill." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Skill ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:skillDescription a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "description" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this skill does and when to use it." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Skill ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:skillInvocability a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "invocability" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who can invoke this skill: 'user' (slash command), 'agent' (auto-triggered), 'both'." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Skill ;
    rdfs:range soul:SkillInvocabilityValue .

soul:skillAllowedTools a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "allowedTools" ;
    rdfs:comment "Space-separated list of tools the skill may use without confirmation." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Skill ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

soul:skillArgumentHint a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "argumentHint" ;
    rdfs:comment "Hint shown during autocomplete (e.g. '[read|write|status]')." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Skill ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# --- Pursuit ---
# What the soul is motivated to wake up and work on. A project is a pursuit;
# an interest is a pursuit; a research thread is a pursuit. The anchor class:
# Notes and Explorations link TO a Pursuit, and the graph answers "everything
# on this pursuit" through those inbound links — no category fields anywhere.

soul:Pursuit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Pursuit" ;
    rdfs:comment "Something this soul is drawn to advance — a project, an interest, a thread of curiosity. The body is a LIVING statement (what pulls, what you're currently wondering), rewritten in place as the pursuit develops. Boundary: a pursuit is something you ADVANCE; a practice is something you KEEP — habits and routines are not pursuits and do not belong here. Renamed from Interest at v0.9.0 (a disposition became a commitment with direction)." .

soul:pursuitId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "pursuitId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this pursuit (== filename)." ;
    rdfs:domain soul:Pursuit ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .


# =============================================================================
# DEPRECATED — retired CLASSES, kept declared so history can replay.
#
# SCOPE, narrowed by Rob 2026-08-20: this appendix is for CLASSES.
# A retired PROPERTY is DELETED outright — do not add one here.
#
# The asymmetry is mechanical, not stylistic, and it is why one rule cannot
# cover both. Replay derives a triple's SUBJECT from the class (+ its id) and
# its PREDICATE from the frontmatter key text (`{kit_ns}{prop_seg}`,
# nquad.rs:953). Only the subject side consults this file.
#   - Delete a CLASS → the subject can no longer be derived. Historical facts
#     can be neither re-asserted NOR retracted; ghost Things strand in the
#     now-graph and a full rebuild silently erases that class's whole life.
#     That is the v0.9.1 incident, root-caused by W4R3Z on TR1P.L3X's store.
#   - Delete an ORDINARY PROPERTY → nothing moves. The predicate IRI is built
#     from the key the author typed, so every historical triple resolves to
#     exactly the same IRI it always did. What is lost is governance (shape,
#     enum, type check). Verified 2026-08-20 by authoring a key no ttl has ever
#     declared: it reached the graph intact, on the right subject, under the
#     right predicate, with one warning.
#
# THE EXCEPTION, and it is the whole reason this paragraph got rewritten one
# day after it shipped: **property deletion is safe because nothing READS the
# declaration — except where something does, and identity is where something
# does.**
#
# An `<class>Id` property is not ordinary. `derive_file_subjects` gates the
# convention lane on the declared property set (`nquad.rs:483`): if
# `soul:noteId` is not declared, every Note that anchors by convention returns
# File-only subjects — `thing_uri: None`. Not an error, not a lost field. The
# document's whole fact set silently demotes from the Thing plane to the File
# plane, which is the v0.9.1 shape wearing a property's clothes.
#
# So an id property may only be deleted once NOTHING depends on the convention
# lane, and that is a MEASUREMENT, never a judgement call: zero documents
# fleet-wide still anchored by per-class id alone, checked on the day of the
# cut. W4R3Z adds the other half — the same commit must remove the fallback
# LANE from the code, so ontology and reader can never disagree about whether
# the convention still exists. (Confirmed in code by W4R3Z 2026-08-22, after
# this author asserted the unqualified version here on 2026-08-20 and shipped
# it. The warning text for the demotion had already fired in front of me during
# an unrelated probe the same day; I read it and did not connect it.)
#
# The general lesson, worth more than the specific rule: when a safety property
# rests on "nothing reads X," the work is enumerating the readers — not
# restating the property with more confidence.
#
# So the cost of keeping a dead property was never replay safety — it was a
# retired field going on being offered to authors in the generated template
# (the emitter filters deprecated CLASSES via `class_gets_folder`; it has no
# equivalent for properties). Deprecating a property bought nothing and taxed
# every author who opened the form. Hence: delete.
#
# Nothing below may be used for new documents; the save gate warns on sight.
# Nothing below may be used for new documents; the save gate warns on sight.
#
# AUTHORING PROCEDURE — when retiring a class, STRIP `git-lex:foldered true`
# as you move it down here. Declare it minimally: owl:deprecated, isReplacedBy,
# label, comment. Every entry below complies.
#
# This is now belt AND suspenders. The suspenders are code: git-lex's
# `ontology::class_gets_folder` (foldered AND NOT deprecated) is the single
# predicate the folder audit, the template emitter, init's scaffolding, and
# the cross-kit registry all dispatch through — so a retired class that KEEPS
# the flag still gets no folder and no template (git-lex 470f3a9, 2026-08-08).
# Before that hoist, two gates computed the rule separately and agreed only
# because this author never left `foldered` on a deprecated class; the first
# tired slip would have rebuilt retired folders and fresh key-list templates
# on every seat at kit-update, re-inviting authorship into vocabulary a fleet
# session had just evacuated. Keep stripping the flag anyway: the ttl should
# state what it means, not lean on a reader to be careful for it.
# (Found by w3blord's residue sweep; see field notes §2j.)
# =============================================================================

